# Asymptotic Calculus Catalogue — falsification infrastructure
# (Spike #28 §10 PR #447 + §11 catalog scope inventory).
#
# Each line is one MPR row data block (NDJSON).
# Lines starting with "#" or empty lines are skipped by the
# literature_curated adapter per `srmech.amsc.format.read_ndjson`.
#
# Row shape: see row.schema.json for the formal schema.
# Per-row fields:
#   row_label    : human-readable identifier
#   row_type     : taxonomy ("partial_sum_truncation" only at rc6)
#   kind         : transcendental being computed ("exp" only at rc6)
#   x_num        : numerator p of input x = p/q
#   x_den        : denominator q of input x = p/q (positive)
#   num_terms    : truncation parameter N >= 0
#   expected_num : ground-truth numerator of S_N(p/q) in lowest terms
#   expected_den : ground-truth denominator of S_N(p/q) in lowest terms
#   source_apostol: Apostol *Mathematical Analysis* Theorem 12.20 (Lagrange)
#   source_bishop : Bishop *Foundations of Constructive Analysis* §2
#   chain_id     : the operator_chain that produces this row's output
#   entered_locally_at: date catalog row added (UTC)
#
# Coverage at rc6: 12 rows covering x in {0, ±1/2, ±1, ±2} at
# N in {5, 10, 15}. Future rcs add trig (sin / cos / tan), log,
# atan, Bessel, Γ, ζ, and calculus ops (forward_difference / riemann_sum).
#
# All `expected_*` values were produced by running srmech v0.4.1rc6's
# `srmech.amsc.rational.exp_series_truncate` at catalog author time
# (2026-05-16). The catalog's purpose is to FALSIFY any drift in that
# function: CI re-runs the chain at consumer time and bit-exact-compares.

